# Spring Boot Auto-Configuration

## What it is
Auto-configuration is one of Spring Boot's most powerful features. It automatically configures your Spring application based on the dependencies on your classpath. For example, adding `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` causes Spring Boot to automatically configure a `DataSource`, persistence manager, and related components.

## How it works
1. Spring Boot scans the classpath and applies relevant auto-configurations.
2. It discovers them via the `spring.factories` file in the `META-INF` directory of each starter.
3. Auto-configurations are ordinary `@Configuration` classes.

`@EnableAutoConfiguration` is the annotation that turns this on. It is one of the three annotations composed into `@SpringBootApplication`, alongside `@Configuration` (source of bean definitions) and `@ComponentScan` (component scanning).

## Conditional annotations (documented example)
```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnClass({DataSource.class, JdbcTemplate.class})
@EnableConfigurationProperties(JdbcProperties.class)
@AutoConfigureAfter(DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
public class JdbcTemplateAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate(DataSource dataSource) {
        return new JdbcTemplate(dataSource);
    }
}
```
- `@ConditionalOnClass`: the configuration applies only if `DataSource` and `JdbcTemplate` are on the classpath.
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`: the bean is created only if you haven't defined one yourself, so you can always override the default.
- `@EnableConfigurationProperties` and `@AutoConfigureAfter` appear in the example, but the documentation doesn't explain them in detail. By name, the latter orders this configuration after `DataSourceAutoConfiguration`.

## Config demo (executed)
I ran a Python simulation of the conditional logic above (this models the decision logic only; it is not real Spring code):
- **Scenario 1** (required classes present, no user bean): Created default JdbcTemplate bean
- **Scenario 2** (user defines their own JdbcTemplate): Skipped, so the user-defined bean overrides the default
- **Scenario 3** (JdbcTemplate missing from classpath): Auto-config skipped because the required classes are not on the classpath

## Database query results
I queried the documentation database for example docs. A search for Spring Boot auto-configuration documents returned no results. A broader search for Spring-related docs returned only general Spring Framework docs (IoC container, dependency injection, bean scopes and lifecycle, profiles, MVC, REST API development), with no dedicated auto-configuration example files. So the example above comes from the documentation corpus (RAG) rather than from a dedicated database entry.

## Troubleshooting
If auto-configuration isn't working, first verify that the required starter dependency is declared in your `pom.xml` or `build.gradle`.

